"use strict";(self.webpackChunkwebsite=self.webpackChunkwebsite||[]).push([["7204"],{8654(e,n,t){t.r(n),t.d(n,{metadata:()=>r,default:()=>c,frontMatter:()=>s,contentTitle:()=>i,toc:()=>u,assets:()=>l});var r=JSON.parse('{"id":"reference/configure/howtos/createFleetManagementGroup","title":"How to Create Fleet Management Groups","description":"almsc \xb7 imported from live Hugo on 2026-10-07. Guides stay latest. Original page.","source":"@site/docs/reference/configure/howtos/createFleetManagementGroup.md","sourceDirName":"reference/configure/howtos","slug":"/reference/configure/howtos/createFleetManagementGroup","permalink":"/personal-av-docs/docs/reference/configure/howtos/createFleetManagementGroup","draft":false,"unlisted":false,"editUrl":"https://github.com/andreyjv/personal-av/tree/docs/documentation-revamp/PRD_documentation-revamp/website/docs/reference/configure/howtos/createFleetManagementGroup.md","tags":[],"version":"current","sidebarPosition":99,"frontMatter":{"title":"How to Create Fleet Management Groups","sidebar_label":"How to Create Fleet Management Groups","sidebar_position":99,"edition":"almsc","source":"https://doc.airvantage.net/almsc/reference/configure/howtos/createFleetManagementGroup","format":"md"}}'),o=t(4848),a=t(8453);let s={title:"How to Create Fleet Management Groups",sidebar_label:"How to Create Fleet Management Groups",sidebar_position:99,edition:"almsc",source:"https://doc.airvantage.net/almsc/reference/configure/howtos/createFleetManagementGroup",format:"md"},i,l={},u=[];function p(e){let n={a:"a",p:"p",strong:"strong",...(0,a.R)(),...e.components};return(0,o.jsxs)(o.Fragment,{children:[(0,o.jsxs)(n.p,{children:[":::note Edition\n",(0,o.jsx)(n.strong,{children:"almsc"})," \xb7 imported from live Hugo on 2026-10-07. Guides stay latest. ",(0,o.jsx)(n.a,{href:"https://doc.airvantage.net/almsc/reference/configure/howtos/createFleetManagementGroup",children:"Original page"}),".\n:::"]}),"\n",(0,o.jsx)(n.p,{children:"How to Create Fleet Management Groups\nFleet Management Groups\nA system can only belong to one Fleet Management Group. Fleet Management Group membership cannot change unless you manually move the device from one group to another.\nFleet Management Groups are best suited to keeping your routers updated with common settings (using linked configuration templates) and software. This is done by setting group policies (rules) that define what software and settings that routers in a group can run.\nCreating Fleet Management Groups\nCreating a Fleet Management Group includes these steps:\nAdding and naming the Group\nAdding\nrules\nto the Group. You can use rules to apply consistent configurations across devices in your group.\nHeartbeat\n\u2013Sets the AirVantage check-in status report frequency for all devices in the group\nInstall Firmware\n\u2013Installs the selected operating system firmware version on the selected device model. Create one Install Firmware rule for each device model in the group.\nApply template\n\u2013Applies the selected device template on the selected device model. Create one Apply template rule for each device model in the group.\nAdding Devices (Systems) to the Group\nAdding users to the Group\nTo create a new Fleet Management Group:\nGo to\nConfigure > Groups\n.\nSelect the type of Group to create:\nFleet Management\n.\nClick\nAdd group\u2026\n. The\nNew group\npanel appears.\nIf required, under\nParent\n, click to select a Parent Group for the new Group.\nEnter a\nName\nfor the Group.\nClick\nCreate\n(or click\nRevert\nto start over).\nNow that you\u2019ve created the Group, you can start adding rules.\nTo add Group rules:\nClick\nAdd rule\u2026\nSelect one or more rules.\nAfter selecting one instance of each rule, the new group panel will appear as below.\nConfigure the selected rules. For more information, see\nFleet Management Group Rules\n.\nOptional: Click the up/down buttons\nto change the order of the selected rules. When rules are applied (see\nApplying Rules\n), they are applied in order, top to bottom.\nClick\nto remove a rule.\nClick\nSave\n.\nTo add devices to a Group:\nGo to\nMonitor > Systems\n.\nSelect the systems you want to add.\nClick\nMore general & life cycle actions\nand go to\nGroups > Add to groups\n.\nIn the Systems > Add Group panel, click\nFleet management\n.\nSelect the desired Group.\nClick\nAdd\n.\nOn the confirmation message, select an option:\nAdd and apply rules\u2013adds the devices and applies the rules immediately (update the device firmware, for example)\nAdd only\u2013adds the devices without applying rules\nCancel\u2013return to the group selection panel\nCreating a Child Group\nThe number of parent/child group levels is limited to 10. For example:\nParent (level 1)\nChild/Parent 1 (level 2)\nChild/Parent 1.1 (level 3)\nChild/Parent 1.1.1 (level 4)\nChild/Parent 1.1.1.1 (level 5)\nChild/Parent 1.1.1.1.1 (level 6)\nChild/Parent 1.1.1.1.1.1 (level 7)\nChild/Parent 1.1.1.1.1.1.1 (level 8)\nChild/Parent 1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1 (level 9)\nChild 1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1 (level 10)\nChild Fleet Management Groups are empty when created, and you must manually add rules and devices to them.\nTo create a Child Group:\nGo to\nConfigure > Groups\n.\nSelect the type of Group to create:\nFleet Management\n.\nIn the desired Parent Group\u2019s\nName\ncolumn, click\nAdd\n.\nThe New group panel appears, showing you the parent Group.\nAdd a name for the new child Group, and configure it as described in\nCreating Fleet Management Groups\nabove.\nFleet Management Group Rules\nHeartbeat\nThe Heartbeat rule sets how often the router checks in to AirVantage and sends its default status report.\nThe default setting is 15 minutes. This is also the minimum setting, but you can set the Heartbeat to longer periods in units of minutes, hours, days and weeks.\nThis rule is applied to every device in the group.\nInstall firmware\nApplying the Install firmware rule updates routers with a selected firmware release.\nEach router model in the group needs to be added to the Install firmware rule.\nTo configure the Install firmware rule:\nClick\nAdd device model\u2026\n.\nSelect the desired AirLink router model.\nClick\nSelect a firmware\u2026\nSelect the desired firmware version.\nIf you have other router models in the Group for which you want to install firmware, return to step 1.\nClick\nSave\n.\nApply template\nApplying the Apply template rule updates routers with a selected device template. For more information, see\nHow to create and apply a template\n.\nEach router model in the group needs to be added to the Apply template rule.\nTo configure the Apply template rule:\nClick\nAdd device model\u2026\n.\nSelect the desired AirLink router model.\nClick\nSelect a template\u2026\nSelect the desired template.\nIf you have other router models in the Group to which you want to apply templates, return to step 1.\nClick\nSave\n.\nEnsure that the templates that you select are compatible with any Install firmware rules selected. The device names and firmware versions in the firmware names and the template names should match.\nCheck Compliance\nUnder the Compliance tab, you can check which devices are complying with the Group rules. For example, the Compliance tab indicates which devices are not running the selected firmware version or have not had the selected template applied.\nDevices\u2019 compliance status is not automatically reported when you add devices to a Group.\nClick\nCheck Compliance\nto generate device compliance status for a Group.\nThe compliance results are also summarized in the main Groups table, in the Rule compliance column.\nIf any devices are not compliant, you can:\nchange the Group rules. When you save the change, you can apply the updated rules to the devices\napply the group rules to bring the device into compliance. See Applying Group Rules below.\nApplying Group Rules\nTo apply Group rules:\nSelect the Group in the main Groups table.\nClick\nActions > Apply group rules\n.\nIn the Groups panel that appears, select the rules to apply. You do not need to apply all the Group rules.\nClick\nApply\n.\nYou can apply rules to only one Group at a time.\nRules are applied in order, top to bottom. In the example above, the device will receive a firmware update, then have a template applied.\nFirmware rules may fail to apply because the devices are running much older firmware versions that require an interim upgrade before upgrading to the target version.\nA template application may fail because the template is not compatible with the firmware version running on the device. Ensure that your\nInstall firmware\nand\nApply template\nrules are using the same firmware version.\nOperations may fail if the device does not check in within the timeout threshold\nAdd Users to a Group\nAdministrators and users with \u201CGroups Write\u201D permission can add users to a Group. You can add users from your company and any partnered companies. The process of adding users also includes adding profiles to those users to control who can view or edit the Group, and control what users can do to the systems within the Group.\nBefore starting, confirm that your users exist (see\nAdministration > Users\n) and that the profiles for those users are configured (see\nAdministration > Profiles\n). For more information, see\nAdministration\nand\nManaging Profiles\n.\nTo add users to a Group:\nWhen editing a Group, click\nAccess\n.\nIn the Access tab, click\nAdd users\u2026\n.\nIn the Add users panel, under\nCompany\n, select the company from which you want to add users. Click\nFilter\u2026\nto search for a specific partnered company.\nUnder\nUsers\n, select one or more users. Click\nSelect users\u2026\nto search for a specific user.\nTo select more users from a different company, return to Step 3.\nUnder\nProfiles\n, select one or more profiles that will control what the users can do within that Group. For example,\nGroups Read\nor\nGroups Read/Write\n. In this example, you could also select\nConfiguration Management\nso that the users can configure systems in that Group.\nCommon profile combinations:\n\u201CGroups Read\u201D + \u201CSystems View\u201D for view-only users\n\u201CGroups Read/Write\u201D + \u201CSystems View\u201D + \u201COperations\u201D for full management\nClick\nAdd\n.\nVerify that the users now appear in the Access tab with assigned profiles.\nThe next time those users log in, they will be able to view only the Groups to which they have access and be able to take actions according to their assigned rights.\nUsers require both Groups\nRead\nand\nWrite\naccess to view Groups and modify them.\nUser access can be affected by whether a Group is a parent or child Group.\nUsers added to a Group will have access to that Group\u2019s child Groups using the same profiles they were assigned in the parent Group. You can add additional profiles for a user in the child Group. However, if a user wants to modify a child Group\u2019s definition, then they must have Group Write permission in the immediate parent Group.\nDeleting Users\nTo delete a user:\nWhen editing a group, click\nAccess\n.\nIn the Access tab, under Actions, click\n.\nClick\nConfirm\n. The user loses access immediately.\nYou cannot delete a user or remove a profile from the user if that user or profile exists in a parent Group. If a user has access via a parent Group, remove them from the parent Group instead.\nEdit Groups\nTo edit Groups, you must have Groups Read/Write access, AND the Group cannot be a top-level Group. Top-level Groups can be edited only by System administrators.\nTo edit an existing group:\nGo to\nConfigure > Groups\n.\nClick the desired Fleet Management group. The group details appear.\nIf you have the required group access rights, you can edit the following options:\nEdit the group\nName\nEdit or add a\nRule\nIf you have the required group access rights, you can edit the Group users.\nClick\nSave\nor click\nRevert\nto cancel any changes."})]})}function c(e={}){let{wrapper:n}={...(0,a.R)(),...e.components};return n?(0,o.jsx)(n,{...e,children:(0,o.jsx)(p,{...e})}):p(e)}},8453(e,n,t){t.d(n,{R:()=>s,x:()=>i});var r=t(6540);let o={},a=r.createContext(o);function s(e){let n=r.useContext(a);return r.useMemo(function(){return"function"==typeof e?e(n):{...n,...e}},[n,e])}function i(e){let n;return n=e.disableParentContext?"function"==typeof e.components?e.components(o):e.components||o:s(e.components),r.createElement(a.Provider,{value:n},e.children)}}}]);